{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "What is the objective of this analysis — are we maximizing total export revenue (sum of each product's production quantity times its world-market price), or is there another goal such as maximizing net trade balance or minimizing imports?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice regarding the optimization objective, presenting alternatives within one coherent question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "How exactly is domestic GDP defined in this model — is it the total value of all four products produced (steel, engines, electronics, plastic) valued at their world-market prices, or does it exclude some products or subtract the value of imported goods used in production?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy definition regarding the calculation of domestic GDP, presenting mutually exclusive options within one sentence."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Are the four products (steel, engines, electronics, plastic) all produced domestically in Carelland, or are some of them only available for import and not produced at all?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains a single clarification question asking for the production status of the four products."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "The input table lists \"Klunz imported goods\" as an input for each product — is there a limit on how much imported goods Carelland can obtain, or is that input effectively unlimited (i.e., no constraint on imports)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding whether the imported goods input is constrained or unlimited."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "The input table lists quantities like \"0.02 engines\" per unit of steel and \"0.8 steel\" per unit of engines — are these intermediate inputs drawn from Carelland's own domestic production of those goods (so they must be produced in sufficient quantity), or are they simply fixed resource costs that do not require the corresponding product to be produced?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains a single clarification question asking for a policy choice regarding the nature of the intermediate inputs (domestic production vs. fixed resource costs)."
}

